      "id": 1,
      "text": "Linton Hall takes its name from the former Linton Hall Military School, a Catholic boys’ academy that operated on the site from 1922 to 1974.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.trawellino.com/destinations/united-states/virginia_linton-hall",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "A historic one‑room schoolhouse built in 1911 still stands in Linton Hall, a rare reminder of the town’s rural past.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.trawellino.com/destinations/united-states/virginia_linton-hall",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Linton family cemetery, located inside the Braemar subdivision, remains unclaimed after Civil War records were lost during the courthouse fire.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18,
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linton_Hall%2C_Virginia",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Benedictine nuns founded Linton Hall School on the former Linton's Ford Plantation, turning the estate into a center for educating poor boys and girls.",
